("With one or two fights a day that is close to once per fight, which makes it better at this table than usual. It still offers less than Dimensional Disappearance at level 10.", "With one or two fights in a day that comes close to once in each fight, which makes it better for Cyrus than it usually is. It still offers less than Dimensional Disappearance at level 10."),
("Sprinting Spellstrike is a core part of Cyrus's design, both as the thematic and exciting option the player wants and for what it adds to his mobility and damage. He moves in a straight line",
 "Sprinting Spellstrike is central to the way Cyrus fights from level 12, because it adds to his mobility and his damage at once. He moves in a straight line"),
("It combines well with Lightning Bolt and Blazing Fissure, and with Living Thunderbolt, whose fly Speed Sprinting can use and which damages every creature he passes through.", "It combines well with Lightning Bolt and Blazing Fissure, and also with Living Thunderbolt, whose fly Speed the movement can use and which damages every creature he passes through."),
("At this table, with one or two fights a day and time to rest between them, ordinary Refocus already restores most or all of the pool before the next fight. It would only matter on long dungeon days with several fights in a row.",
 "With one or two fights in a day and time to rest between them, an ordinary Refocus already restores most or all of the pool before the next fight. The feat would matter only on long days with several fights in succession."),
("Arcane Cascade's damage-type switch and a varied set of prepared spells usually solve the same problem.", "Arcane Cascade's choice of damage type and a varied set of prepared spells usually solve the same problem."),
("Detect Magic is already a cantrip and the party's casters have it, so a class feat is too high a price.", "Detect Magic is already a cantrip, and one that other casters among his allies have, so a class feat is too high a price."),
("Cast in the first round, it gives him an extra action every turn of the fight, close to a permanent Haste on himself. It doesn't stack with Haste, which frees the party's Haste for another melee ally.", "Cast in the first round, it gives him an extra action in every turn of the fight, which is close to having Haste on himself whenever he wants it. The two do not combine, so an ally's Haste can go to someone else who fights in melee."),
("Arcane Shroud turns every entry into Arcane Cascade into a short buff: Cyrus picks one of his three chosen spells each time and keeps it until the end of his next turn. By 16 he is often quickened from Haste, Hasted Assault or an ally, which covers his movement and leaves a normal action free to re-enter the stance. Invisibility is the strongest pick: re-entered as the last action of a turn, it forces a DC 11 flat check on every attack against him until his next hostile action, and the Strike that ends it is made while he is hidden, so the target is off-guard and Laughing Shadow applies.",
 "Arcane Shroud gives Cyrus a brief spell effect each time he enters Arcane Cascade. He selects one of his three chosen spells each time and keeps it until the end of his next turn. By 16 he is often quickened by Haste, Hasted Assault or an ally, which provides his movement and leaves an ordinary action free for entering the stance again. Invisibility is the strongest of the choices. When he enters the stance with the last action of a turn, every attack against him needs a DC 11 flat check until his next hostile action, and the Strike that ends the invisibility is made while he is hidden, so the target is off-guard and Laughing Shadow's extra damage applies."),
("Two limits matter: he gets one of the three spells per entry, not all three, and stances can only be entered in encounter mode, so it gives no out-of-combat invisibility for scouting. Re-entering is also limited to once per round by the stance trait.",
 "It has two limits that matter. He gets one of the three spells each time, not all three, and a stance can be entered only during an encounter, so the feat gives no invisibility for scouting outside combat. The stance trait also limits him to entering it once a round."),
("A Dispel Magic scroll or the party's casters handle buffs and wards more cheaply.", "A scroll of Dispel Magic, or another caster among his allies, deals with enemy spells and wards at less cost."),
("Fleet adds 5 feet to Cyrus's Speed, and almost everything this build does scales with Speed. Dimensional Assault teleports up to half his Speed before level 9 and his full Speed after, so Fleet extends every teleport; it also extends every Stride to a flanking square and every approach before a Sprinting Spellstrike. It stacks with Laughing Shadow's bonus and with boots, and it costs nothing to use. For a reach skirmisher whose turns depend on reaching the right square, no other general feat adds as much to every fight.",
 "Fleet adds 5 feet to Cyrus's Speed, and almost everything he does depends on Speed. Dimensional Assault teleports him up to half his Speed before level 9 and his full Speed afterwards, so Fleet lengthens every teleport, as well as every Stride to a flanking square and every approach before a Sprinting Spellstrike. It adds to Laughing Shadow's bonus and to the bonus from boots, and it costs nothing to use. His turns depend on reaching the right square, so no other general feat adds as much to every fight."),
("Expert Perception from level 1 to 8 is eight levels ahead of the class. Perception is his initiative, so in the campaign's hard fights it decides whether Cyrus trips or debuffs the key enemy before it acts. It is also the roll for spotting ambushes and hidden creatures in exploration, and for Sense Motive in the social scenes that fill most sessions. The class catches up at 9, but the feat then takes him to master at 17, so it keeps paying off at both ends of the campaign.",
 "Canny Acumen makes Cyrus an expert in Perception from level 1, eight levels before his class would. He rolls Perception for initiative, so in a hard fight it helps to decide whether he trips or weakens the most dangerous enemy before it acts. It is also the roll for noticing ambushes and hidden creatures during exploration, and for Sense Motive in conversation. His class reaches expert at 9, but the feat then takes him to master at 17, so it remains useful at both low and high levels."),
("That is how Cyrus keeps two strong Lores while still raising Athletics and Arcana. Warfare Lore reaching master at 7 is what unlocks Improvise Strategy, so this feat supports his combat plan as well as his scholarly role.", "That is how Cyrus keeps two strong Lores while still raising Athletics and Arcana. Improvise Strategy requires Warfare Lore at master, which he reaches at 7, so this feat supports the way he fights as well as his learning."),
("He fights from inside the enemy line, ends turns next to the target and spends actions on Spellstrike rather than defence, so the hard fights are where he is most likely to drop. Extra Hit Points every fight, plus a better chance of stabilising when he does go down, make it one of the most reliable general feats for him. It does nothing else, which is the only reason it isn't higher.",
 "He fights among the enemy, ends his turns close to his target and spends his actions on Spellstrike instead of defence, so it is in hard fights that he is most likely to fall. More Hit Points in every fight, and a better chance of stabilising when he does fall, make it one of the most dependable general feats for him. It does nothing else, which is the only reason it is not graded higher."),
("Acting before enemies lets Cyrus trip or debuff a key enemy before the rest of the party acts, and reposition before the enemy can surround the party.", "Acting before the enemy lets Cyrus trip or weaken its most dangerous member before his allies act, and take up a good position before his side can be surrounded."),
("Pairing Arcana with Academia Lore or Society means far fewer blank results when he identifies an enemy, an artefact or a historical clue. It also backs up Magus's Analysis in combat, though the reaction competes with Reactive Strike there.",
 "With Arcana paired with Academia Lore or Society, he learns nothing far less often when he tries to identify an enemy, an artefact or a historical clue. It also gives Magus's Analysis a second chance in combat, although there it uses the reaction he keeps for Reactive Strike."),
("Gold is the tightest resource in this build, with runes, Aspera's stave and invested items all competing for it, so halving the cost of every spell Cyrus adds to his spellbook is a meaningful saving across the campaign. The 10-minute learning time matters as much: downtime may be rare, and this lets him copy a scroll or a captured spellbook during a short rest. The downtime option to learn spells instead of earning income is a bonus when downtime does come.",
 "Gold is what Cyrus is shortest of, because runes, Aspera's stave and his invested items all have to be paid for, so halving the cost of every spell he adds to his spellbook saves a great deal over time. The 10-minute learning time matters as much, because it lets him copy a scroll or a captured spellbook during a short rest instead of waiting for free days. When he does have free days, the option to learn spells in place of earning income is a further benefit."),
("Off-guard turns on Laughing Shadow's increased damage, and clumsy makes the following Trip easier. It is the opening move of his signature turn: Improvise Strategy, then Trip, then Dimensional Assault.",
 "The off-guard condition brings Laughing Shadow's increased damage, and the clumsy condition makes the Trip that follows easier. It begins the turn he most often wants to take, which is Improvise Strategy, then a Trip, then Dimensional Assault."),
("It only works when the party has time to scout, and Incredible Initiative improves his initiative more reliably.", "It works only when there is time to scout beforehand, and Incredible Initiative improves his initiative more reliably."),
("Critical failures on Recall Knowledge give false information, which is worse than nothing when the party acts on it. As the party's scholar, with several Lores from Additional Lore and Aspera's gifts, he rolls these checks often in both investigation and combat, so the protection is used most sessions.",
 "A critical failure on Recall Knowledge gives false information, which is worse than none when others act on it. As the scholar among his allies, with several Lores from Additional Lore and Aspera's gifts, he makes these checks often in both investigation and combat, so the protection is in regular use."),
("built on an Intelligence skill rather than Charisma.", "based on an Intelligence skill instead of on Charisma."),
("In a roleplay-heavy campaign with recurring noble and academic contacts it is a real source of information, access and introductions. Its value depends on how often the party returns to places where he has a network.",
 "Where he has noble and academic acquaintances whom he meets repeatedly, it is a real source of information, access and introductions. Its value depends on how often he returns to places where he knows people."),
("From level 12 many bosses are Huge or larger, and without this feat his main setup action stops working against them, so it keeps his tripping style effective late in the campaign.",
 "From level 12 many of the most dangerous enemies are Huge or larger, and without this feat he could not Trip them at all, so it keeps his tripping effective at high level."),
("It helps on vertical maps, but Dimensional Assault and Time Jump cover most of the same movement. It is the base for Cloud Jump at 18, which is where the pair becomes worth using.",
 "It helps where a fight involves height, but Dimensional Assault and Time Jump provide most of the same movement. It is the prerequisite for Cloud Jump at 18, and it is with that feat that the two become worth using."),
("which supports the party's Will-targeting casters.", "which helps allies whose spells target Will."),
("That makes Cyrus the party's answer to every magical item, effect, ward or creature, using his best skill. In a campaign with a lot of investigation, it saves the party from depending on whoever happens to have the matching skill.",
 "That lets Cyrus answer questions about every magical item, effect, ward or creature with his best skill. Where there is a great deal to investigate, it means the answer no longer depends on whoever happens to have the matching skill."),
